.TH SG 1 "2026-08-04" "small_git 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sg \- small_git: a git-object-format-compatible version control tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sg
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B sg
.RB ( -h | --help | help )
.br
.B sg
.RB ( -v | --version | version )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sg
is a from-scratch, pure C implementation of a version control tool that
reads and writes the same on-disk object format, packfile format, and index
format as
.BR git (1).
A repository initialized by
.B sg
can be operated on by the real
.B git
binary and vice versa, in the same
.B .git
directory, as long as
.B sg
specific opt-in features (content-defined chunking; see
.B chunk-info
below and the
.B FILES
section) are not in use.
.PP
.B sg
implements a focused subset of git's porcelain and plumbing commands. It
does not attempt to be a drop-in replacement for every git flag or
subcommand; only the commands and options listed below are recognized. In
particular, most long-form git options (e.g.
.BR "git log --oneline" ,
.BR "git commit --amend" )
have no
.B sg
equivalent unless explicitly documented here.
.PP
Every subcommand that touches the working directory or index requires being
run inside a repository (a directory containing, or with an ancestor
containing, a
.B .git
directory), the same discovery rule git itself uses.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.B sg init
.RI [ directory ]
.PP
Creates a new repository. If
.I directory
is given it is created (if necessary) and initialized there; otherwise the
current directory is used. Prints
.RI "\(dqInitialized empty Git repository in " dir "/.git/\(dq" .
.SS hash-object
.B sg hash-object
.RB [ -w ]
.RB [ -t
.IR type ]
.RB [ --stdin ]
.I file
.PP
Computes the object id of a file's content (or, with
.BR --stdin ", of standard input)."
.B -t
.I type
selects the object type used to compute the hash (default
.BR blob ).
.B -w
additionally writes the object into the repository's object store as a
loose object.
.I file
is required unless
.B --stdin
is given. The whole input is read into memory before hashing; there is no
streaming mode.
.SS cat-file
.B sg cat-file
.RB ( -t | -s | -p )
.I object
.PP
Inspects a single object by its full 40-character hex id (abbreviated ids
are not accepted).
.B -t
prints the object's type,
.B -s
prints its size in bytes, and
.B -p
pretty-prints its content: for a
.B tree
object, each entry is printed as
.IR "mode type hash\etname" ;
for
.B blob
and
.B commit
objects, the object's raw stored bytes are written to stdout unmodified.
If the object is a blob whose content is an
.BR sg -internal
chunked-storage pointer (see
.BR chunk-info ),
a note is printed to stderr explaining this; stdout still receives the
pointer's own raw text, never the reassembled file content \(em
.B cat-file
never resolves chunk pointers.
.SS add
.B sg add
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ -- ]
.I path
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Stages files into the index. Each
.I path
must resolve inside the repository; paths outside the repository are
rejected. A
.I path
naming a directory is walked recursively (entries are processed in sorted
order), so
.B "sg add ."
stages the whole tree from the current directory down. The walk never
descends into a
.B .git
directory at any depth, and files matched by the ignore rules described
under
.B .gitignore
below are skipped silently \(em except that an already-tracked file is
always staged, because ignore rules never apply to tracked content.
.PP
Naming an ignored path explicitly on the command line is an error rather
than a silent no-op; pass
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
to stage it anyway. A
.B --
argument ends option parsing, so a file literally named
.B -f
can be staged.
.PP
A
.I path
that no longer exists but is still recorded in the index stages its
deletion. Under a directory argument, the deletion of any tracked file
beneath it is staged the same way, matching
.BR "git add <dir>" .
.PP
Either every named path stages successfully or the index is left
completely untouched: the index is written only after all arguments have
been processed without error.
.PP
Remaining differences from
.BR "git add" :
.IP \(bu 2
.B Symlinks are skipped, not staged.
A symlink argument prints a warning (\(dqis a symlink, skipping
(unsupported)\(dq) and is not added to the index; this is not an error for
the overall command.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Only regular files are staged.
A path that is not a regular file (FIFO, socket, device) is reported as an
error when named explicitly, and skipped during a directory walk. There is
no
.BR "git add -p" ,
no pathspec globbing, and no
.BR "git add -A" /\%\fB-u\fR
distinction.
.IP \(bu 2
If a path currently carries an unresolved merge conflict (stage 1/2/3
entries in the index), staging it clears the conflict and records the
working-directory content as the resolution \(em this is how
.B sg
expects conflicts to be marked resolved during
.B merge
or
.BR rebase .
.PP
If chunked storage is enabled for the repository (see
.B FILES
below) and a file's content is at or above the configured threshold, its
blob is written using content-defined chunking instead of as a single
ordinary blob.
.SS commit
.B sg commit -m
.I message
.PP
Creates a commit from the current index content, updating the current
branch to point at it.
.B -m
.I message
is required; there is no editor-based message flow, no
.BR --amend ,
and no way to sign or author-override a commit via flags (see
.B ENVIRONMENT
for author/committer identity).
.I message
is normalized the same way real git's default
.B --cleanup=whitespace
does: trailing whitespace is stripped from each line, runs of blank lines
are collapsed to one, and leading/trailing blank lines are dropped. If the
result is empty (including an all-whitespace
.IR message ),
.B sg
refuses to create the commit, matching real git.
.PP
Fails if the index has unresolved merge conflicts (stage 1/2/3 entries) or
if a rebase is currently in progress (use
.B "sg rebase --continue"
instead). If a merge is in progress (\c
.B MERGE_HEAD
present) and all conflicts are resolved, the resulting commit is a merge
commit with two parents.
.SS log
.B sg log
.PP
Prints the commit history starting at
.BR HEAD ,
one commit per entry (hash, author, date, message), in the familiar
.B "git log"
default format. Takes no arguments or flags.
.B "sg log"
walks
.B first-parent only
\(em when a merge commit is encountered its other parents are not
themselves visited (though the commit is flagged with a
.B Merge:
line listing all of its parents). There is no
.BR --oneline ,
.BR --graph ,
path filtering, or any other
.B "git log"
option.
.SS status
.B sg status
.PP
Prints the current branch, whether a merge or rebase is in progress (with
next-step hints), and three sections of changes: staged (index vs.
.BR HEAD ),
unstaged (working directory vs. index), and untracked files \(em each
section includes a short hint about the relevant follow-up command, such as
.B add
or
.BR restore .
Unresolved merge conflicts are listed under \(dqUnmerged paths\(dq. Takes no
arguments or flags.
.PP
The untracked list honours the ignore rules described under
.B .gitignore
in
.B FILES
below: ignored files are omitted and ignored directories are not descended
into. Tracked files are never affected by ignore rules. Every untracked
path is listed individually; unlike
.BR "git status" ,
directories are not collapsed. If a directory cannot be read (for instance
a tree nested deeper than the platform's
.BR PATH_MAX ,
which
.B sg
cannot traverse because it composes absolute paths), a warning is printed
to standard error saying the list may be incomplete, rather than the entry
being dropped silently.
.SS diff
.B sg diff
.PP
Shows the difference between the index and the working directory (i.e. the
unstaged changes that
.B "git diff"
would show with no arguments) for every stage-0 index entry, in unified-diff
form. Files whose content contains a NUL byte are reported as \(dqBinary
files ... differ\(dq rather than diffed line-by-line. Entries with an
unresolved merge conflict (stage 1/2/3) are skipped (see
.B status
for those). Takes no arguments or flags; there is no path filtering, no
.BR --staged / --cached ,
and no diffing against an arbitrary commit or ref.
.PP
The line-level diff algorithm is a plain LCS-based implementation: it is
correct but does not replicate git's exact hunk-splitting or context-window
conventions, and always emits a single hunk covering the whole file.
.SS branch
.B sg branch
.RB [ -d | --delete ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ name ]
.PP
With no arguments, lists every branch, one per line, with the current
branch marked by a leading
.BR * .
The listing merges the loose files under
.B refs/heads/
with the entries in
.BR packed-refs ,
so a branch that
.B "git gc"
has packed away is still listed; where both exist the loose value wins,
which is the same precedence
.B sg
uses when resolving a single ref.
.PP
With
.IR name ,
creates a branch pointing at the current
.B HEAD
commit and does
.B not
switch to it (use
.B "sg switch"
for that). Creating fails if the name already exists in either store, if
the repository has no commits yet, or if the name is not a valid ref name
\(em names may not begin with
.BR - ,
begin or end with
.BR / ,
end with
.B .
or
.BR .lock ,
contain
.BR // ,
.BR .. ,
.BR @{ ,
a backslash, whitespace, an ASCII control character, or any of
.BR ~^:?*[ ,
and may not be exactly
.BR HEAD .
Names containing
.B /
(for example
.BR feature/x )
are valid.
.PP
With
.B -d
(or
.BR --delete ),
deletes
.IR name .
Deletion removes the branch from
.B both
the loose file and
.BR packed-refs ;
removing only the loose file would leave a stale packed entry that
silently resurrects the branch at an older commit. The current branch is
never deletable, with or without
.BR --force .
.PP
If the branch is not fully merged into
.B HEAD
its commits would become unreachable, so deletion asks for confirmation
first; the prompt names the branch and its tip. As elsewhere in
.BR sg ,
.B --force
skips the prompt but not the refusal to delete the current branch, and a
non-interactive standard input refuses rather than assuming yes (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
A successful deletion prints the deleted tip's full object id \(em because
.B "sg undo"
snapshots the working tree and index but not refs, that printed id is the
only way to recreate the branch.
.SS tag
.B sg tag
.RB [ -a ]
.RB [ -m
.IR msg ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ name
.RI [ rev ]]
.br
.B sg tag
.RB [ -d | --delete ]
.I name
.PP
With no arguments, lists every tag under
.BR refs/tags/ ,
one per line, sorted byte-wise. As with
.BR "sg branch" ,
the listing merges the loose files with
.B packed-refs
entries, so a tag
.B "git gc"
has packed away is still listed.
.PP
With
.IR name ,
creates a tag named
.I name
pointing at
.I rev
(a commit, branch, or tag name, or a full 40-hex object id; see
.B REVISIONS
below), defaulting to
.B HEAD
if
.I rev
is omitted. Creating fails if a tag by that name already exists, unless
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
is given, in which case the existing tag is overwritten; it also fails if
.I name
is not a valid ref name (the same rules
.B "sg branch"
enforces).
.PP
Without
.B -a
or
.BR -m ,
the tag is
.IR lightweight :
.B refs/tags/\fIname\fR
is written directly as a ref to the target commit, no tag object is
created. With
.B -a
(annotated) or
.B -m
.I msg
(which implies
.BR -a ),
.B sg
instead builds a
.B tag
object \(em recording the target object, its type, the tag name, a tagger
identity/timestamp, and the message \(em writes it as a loose object, and
points
.B refs/tags/\fIname\fR
at that tag object's id instead of the target directly. The message is
normalized the same way as
.BR "sg commit" 's
(real git's default
.BR --cleanup=whitespace ),
but unlike
.BR "sg commit" ,
an empty (or all-whitespace) message is
.I not
rejected \(em it produces a tag object with an empty message segment,
matching real git's own
.B "git tag -a -m ''"
behavior, which is intentionally asymmetric with
.BR "git commit -m ''" .
.B -a
without
.B -m
is a usage error;
.B sg
has no editor integration to prompt for a message (same restriction as
.BR "sg commit" ).
The tagger identity is read from
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
/
.BR GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL ,
defaulting to
.B small_git
/
.BR sg@localhost ,
with a fixed
.B +0000
timezone offset.
.PP
With
.B -d
(or
.BR --delete ),
deletes
.IR name .
As with branch deletion, this removes the tag from
.B both
the loose file and
.B packed-refs
so a stale packed entry cannot resurrect it; deleting a tag that does not
exist is an error, not a silent success. Unlike branch deletion there is no
merge check or confirmation prompt \(em a tag is just a name for an object,
not a line of unique history that could become unreachable.
.B -d
combined with any tag-creation option
.RB ( -a ", " -m ", " --force / -f )
or a second name argument is a usage error, matching real git; this project
does not support deleting more than one tag per invocation, but rejects the
attempt rather than silently deleting only the first name.
.PP
.B sg push
can also transmit tags; see
.B push
below for
.BR --tags .
.SS reset
.B sg reset
.RB [ --soft | --mixed | --hard ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RI [ rev ]
.PP
Moves the current branch to
.I rev
(defaulting to
.BR HEAD ,
so a bare
.B sg reset
re-points the branch at its own current commit \(em a no-op for the branch
itself, but see the index effects of
.B --mixed
below). Fails on a detached
.B HEAD
(the same restriction
.B "sg switch"
and
.B "sg branch"
apply elsewhere: a branch ref is required to move). Unlike
.BR "sg restore" ,
.B reset
has no pathspec form; passing paths after
.B --
is rejected with a pointer to
.B "sg restore --staged"
instead.
.PP
The three modes differ only in how much beyond the branch ref they touch,
and that difference drives three different safety postures:
.TP
.B --soft
Moves only the branch ref.
The index and working directory are left exactly as they are, so nothing
uncommitted is ever at risk \(em there is no confirmation prompt and no
automatic snapshot, matching real git.
.B --soft
refuses outright (exit 1, no changes made) if a merge is unresolved or a
rebase is paused, since silently abandoning that state out from under a
soft reset would be surprising and unrecoverable through
.B --soft
itself; resolve or abort the merge/rebase first.
.TP
.B --mixed
(the default mode when no
.BR --soft / --hard
flag is given) additionally rebuilds the index from
.IR rev 's
tree, discarding whatever was staged. That can lose uncommitted staged
work, but never touches the working directory, so \(em like real git \(em
there is still no interactive confirmation; instead
.B sg
takes an automatic safety snapshot (recoverable via
.BR "sg undo" )
before rewriting the index. If a merge is in progress,
.B --mixed
also clears
.BR MERGE_HEAD ,
ending the merge (matching real git: a later, unrelated
.B "sg commit"
must not silently record a bogus merge commit for a merge the user just
abandoned via reset). It deliberately does
.B not
touch an in-progress rebase's sequencer state \(em matching real git, which
also leaves a paused rebase's on-disk state alone here. No mode of
.B reset
ends a rebase; only
.BR "sg rebase --abort" ,
a run that completes, or
.B "sg undo"
does.
.TP
.B --hard
Additionally overwrites the working directory to match
.IR rev 's
tree, via the same
.B sg_safe_apply_tree
machinery
.B "sg switch"
and
.B "sg restore"
use: an interactive confirmation prompt (bypassable with
.B --force
/
.BR -f ,
real git has no equivalent flag \(em this project's own addition) followed
by an automatic safety snapshot, since this is the one mode that can
overwrite uncommitted working-directory changes outright.
.IP
A paused rebase's sequencer state survives
.BR --hard ,
exactly as it survives
.BR --mixed ,
matching real git: only
.BR rebase 's
own subcommands end a rebase. A conflicted merge's
.B MERGE_HEAD
is cleared, also matching real git.
.PP
Passing more than one of
.BR --soft / --mixed / --hard
is a usage error (exit 1) \(em a deliberate divergence from real git, which
accepts the combination and lets the last flag silently win. An ambiguous
invocation is rejected here rather than guessed at.
.SS switch
.B sg switch
.RB [ -c ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.I branch
.PP
Switches the current branch to
.IR branch ,
updating the working directory and index to match its tip commit.
.B -c
creates
.I branch
(from the current
.BR HEAD )
before switching to it; it is an error if a branch by that name already
exists, or if the current branch has no commits yet.
.PP
While a rebase is paused on a conflict,
.B switch
is refused outright \(em including with
.BR --force ,
which cannot override it. Finish the rebase with
.B "sg rebase --continue"
or end it with
.BR "sg rebase --abort" .
This matches real git, whose
.B "git switch"
likewise refuses (\(dqcannot switch branch while rebasing\(dq) rather than
clobbering the sequencer state, and leaves the new branch un-created when
.B -c
was given.
.PP
Otherwise, if switching would discard uncommitted changes (staged or
unstaged), or would abandon an in-progress merge, the affected paths are
listed and confirmation is required before proceeding (an automatic safety
snapshot is taken first \(em see
.B undo
below); pass
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
to skip the interactive confirmation. A non-interactive stdin (e.g. in a
script) is always treated as \(dqno\(dq unless
.B --force
is given.
.SS restore
.B sg restore
.RB [ --staged ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.I path
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Restores one or more paths. With
.BR --staged ,
unstages
.I path
(resets its index entry to match
.B HEAD
if tracked there, or removes it from the index otherwise) without touching
the working directory. Without
.BR --staged ,
overwrites
.IR path 's
working-directory content with what is currently staged in the index
(not
.BR HEAD ).
.PP
If restoring without
.B --staged
would discard working-directory content that differs from the index, the
affected paths are listed and confirmation is required (with an automatic
safety snapshot taken first), same as
.BR switch .
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
skips the interactive confirmation.
.SS undo
.B sg undo
.RI [ N ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.PP
Without
.IR N ,
lists the repository's automatic safety snapshots (newest first, numbered),
each with its timestamp and a short label describing what created it (e.g.
a
.BR switch ", " restore ", " merge ", or " rebase
that overwrote something). With
.IR N ,
restores the working directory and index to snapshot number
.IR N .
Restoring goes through the same dirty-working-tree confirmation and
automatic snapshot as
.B switch
(so an
.B undo
can itself be undone);
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
skips the confirmation prompt.
.PP
Restoring a snapshot while a rebase is paused
.I does
abandon that rebase's sequencer state, and says so on standard error. This
is the one place
.B sg
deliberately ends a rebase from outside
.BR rebase 's
own subcommands: unlike
.BR switch " and " reset --hard ,
.B undo
has no real-git equivalent to take behaviour from, and resuming a rebase on
top of a working tree that
.B undo
just rewound out from under it would not be meaningful.
.PP
Snapshots are created automatically by
.BR switch ,
.BR restore ,
.BR merge ,
and
.B rebase
whenever they are about to overwrite uncommitted work or abandon an
in-progress merge/rebase; there is no manual \(dqtake a snapshot now\(dq
command. See
.B FILES
for where snapshots are stored.
.SS repack
.B sg repack
.PP
Packs every loose object currently under
.B objects/
into a single new packfile, then removes the now-redundant loose copies.
If there are no loose objects, prints a message and does nothing. Takes no
arguments or flags; there is no delta compression tuning, no
.BR "--window" / "--depth" ,
and no garbage collection of unreachable objects.
.SS merge
.B sg merge
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.I branch
.br
.B sg merge --abort
.PP
Merges
.I branch
into the current branch. If the current branch is a strict ancestor of
.I branch
(or has no commits yet), this is a fast-forward. Otherwise a three-way
merge is attempted using the merge base; conflicting files are written to
the working directory with conflict markers and left staged at stages 1/2/3
for resolution (resolve with
.BR "sg add <file>..." ,
then
.BR "sg commit" ).
.PP
Refuses to start if the working directory is not clean, or if a merge or
rebase is already in progress. A fast-forward or the start of a three-way
merge takes an automatic safety snapshot first (see
.BR undo );
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
only affects the interactive confirmation for a dirty-tree fast-forward, not
whether a snapshot is taken.
.PP
.B --abort
cancels an in-progress merge, restoring the working directory/index to the
pre-merge
.B HEAD
and removing
.BR MERGE_HEAD ,
also taking an automatic safety snapshot first.
.SS merge-base
.B sg merge-base
.I commit-a commit-b
.PP
Prints the hex id of the best common ancestor of the two given commits
(each given as a full 40-character hex object id, not a ref name). If
multiple, mutually unreachable common ancestors exist (a criss-cross merge
history), this is reported as an error rather than picking one arbitrarily.
.SS rebase
.B sg rebase
.I upstream
.br
.B sg rebase --continue
.br
.B sg rebase --skip
.br
.B sg rebase --abort
.PP
Replays the commits unique to the current branch (since its merge base with
.IR upstream )
one at a time on top of
.IR upstream 's
tip, then moves the current branch to point at the result. Exactly one of
.IR upstream ,
.BR --continue ,
.BR --skip ,
or
.B --abort
must be given.
.PP
If
.I upstream
is already an ancestor of the current branch, nothing is replayed
(\(dqup to date\(dq). If the current branch is an ancestor of
.IR upstream ,
this is a pure fast-forward. Rebasing a range that includes a merge commit
is not supported and is reported as an error, with a suggestion to use
.B merge
instead. A commit that becomes empty after replay (its change is already
present upstream) is silently skipped rather than recorded.
.PP
When a replayed commit conflicts, the rebase pauses with conflict markers
and stage 1/2/3 index entries, the same shape as a
.B merge
conflict; resolve with
.BR "sg add <file>..." ,
then
.BR "sg rebase --continue" .
.B --skip
discards the conflicting commit entirely and continues with the next one.
.B --abort
restores the original branch tip and working directory, discarding all
progress made so far.
.PP
Rebase state is stored under
.B .git/sg-rebase/
(see
.BR FILES );
.B sg status
reports an in-progress rebase and the commands above are the only supported
way to leave that state. Requires the current branch (\c
.BR HEAD )
to not be detached. Refuses to start on top of an in-progress merge, and
refuses ordinary
.B commit
while a rebase is in progress.
.SS clone
.B sg clone
.I url
.RI [ directory ]
.PP
Clones a repository served over the git smart HTTP protocol (an
.B http://
or
.B https://
URL) into a new directory. If
.I directory
is omitted, it is derived from the last path segment of
.IR url ,
with a trailing
.B .git
stripped. Refuses to clone into an existing, non-empty directory.
.PP
After fetching, checks out the remote's default branch (preferring its
advertised HEAD symref, then
.BR main ,
then
.BR master ,
then the first advertised branch) and records
.BR "[remote \(dqorigin\(dq]"
and a tracking
.B [branch]
stanza in
.BR .git/config .
If the remote has no branches (only tags, or is entirely empty), the clone
completes without a checkout. There is no support for
.BR git:// ,
.BR ssh:// ,
or local filesystem clone sources, and no
.BR --depth / --branch / --bare
options.
.SS fetch
.B sg fetch
.RI [ remote ]
.PP
Fetches new commits and refs from
.I remote
(default
.BR origin ,
which must already have a
.B url
configured, e.g. by
.BR clone ).
Remote branches are written to
.BR refs/remotes/ \fIremote\fP / ,
tags are written directly to
.BR refs/tags/ .
Neither the current branch nor
.B HEAD
is changed. Have-negotiation is a simplified, capped ancestry walk (not a
full git protocol v2 negotiation) \(em this can only cause some redundant
data to be transferred, never an incorrect result. Takes no other flags;
there is no
.BR --all / --prune / --tags-only .
.SS push
.B sg push
.RI [ remote ]
.RI [ branch | tag ]
.RB [ --tags ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.PP
Pushes a local branch (or tag) to
.I remote
(default
.BR origin )
over the git smart HTTP protocol.
With no name and no
.BR --tags ,
pushes the current branch (and a name is required if
.B HEAD
is detached).
.PP
An explicit
.I name
is looked up as both a branch and a tag; if only one exists, that one is
pushed. If both a branch and a tag have that name, the push is rejected as
ambiguous
.RI ( "src refspec '" name "' matches more than one" ),
matching real git.
.B --tags
pushes every local tag under
.B refs/tags/
in one round trip, instead of a branch.
.PP
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
allows a non-fast-forward update of a pushed branch; without it, a branch
push that is not a fast-forward is rejected. A tag update is never treated
as a fast-forward or merge question at all: if the remote already has a tag
of that name pointing at a different object, the push of that tag is
rejected outright
.RI ( "tag '" name "' already exists on the remote (use --force to overwrite)" )
unless
.B --force
is given, in which case the remote tag is overwritten. With
.BR --tags ,
a rejected tag does not stop the others from being pushed, but the command
still exits non-zero if any tag was rejected. Pushing a tag never creates a
.B refs/remotes/<remote>/<tag>
tracking ref (real git does not either); that only ever happens for a
pushed branch.
.PP
If this repository has ever used chunked storage locally, the push also
attempts to update the remote's
.B refs/sg/chunks
keep-alive ref alongside whatever refs are being pushed (atomically, if the
server advertises that capability) so chunk blobs stay reachable on the
remote too; see
.B FILES
below.
.SS chunk-info
.B sg chunk-info
.IR "path\-or\-blob\-hex"
.PP
Prints diagnostic information about how a file (given as a working-tree
path currently in the index, or as a full 40-character blob hex id) is
stored: whether it is chunked, its original size, its chunk count, and (if
chunked) how many of its chunks are also referenced by some other chunked
blob in the repository (\(dqshared chunks\(dq). This is a read-only
diagnostic command; it does not modify anything.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .gitignore
Per-directory ignore rules, read by
.B status
and
.BR add .
A
.B .gitignore
in any directory applies to that directory and everything below it, and a
deeper file's rules take precedence over a shallower one's; within one
file, the last matching pattern decides. Supported syntax matches
.BR gitignore (5):
comments
.RB ( # ),
negation
.RB ( ! ),
directory-only patterns (trailing
.BR / ),
anchoring (a
.B /
anywhere except at the end anchors the pattern to that file's directory,
otherwise it matches by basename at any depth),
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
character classes
.RB ( [a-c] ,
.BR [!a-c] ),
and
.B **
as a whole path segment. A pattern that excludes a directory buries
everything inside it \(em no negation can re-include content beneath an
excluded directory. Ignore rules never apply to files already tracked in
the index.
.TP
.B .git/info/exclude
Repository-local ignore rules with the same syntax, consulted at lower
precedence than any
.BR .gitignore .
The
.B core.excludesFile
setting and
.B ~/.gitconfig
are
.B not
read, so a global ignore file configured for
.B git
has no effect on
.BR sg .
.TP
.B .git/config
Read for
.B sg
specific settings under an
.B [sg]
section, and for
.B [remote \(dqNAME\(dq]
.B url
(used by
.BR fetch / push ).
Only the repository's own
.B .git/config
is consulted \(em
.B sg
does not read a user-global
.B ~/.gitconfig
or any system-wide git config file.
.RS
.TP
.B chunking
Boolean (\c
.B true
or any other value/absent, treated as false). When true, files added via
.B sg add
whose content is at or above the
.B chunkthreshold
are stored using content-defined chunking instead of as an ordinary blob.
.B Default: false (chunking is off).
.TP
.B chunkthreshold
A positive integer, in bytes. The minimum content size at or above which
.B sg add
chunks a file's blob when
.B chunking
is true. Ignored (and left at its default) if absent, zero, or
unparseable. Default: 4194304 (4 MiB).
.TP
.B everchunked
Boolean, set to
.B true
automatically the first time this repository (locally, or via a
.BR clone / fetch
that pulled in a remote's chunk data) actually produces or receives a
chunked blob. This is a durability marker, not a user-facing setting: once
set, its absence later (e.g. from an edited or reconstructed config) is
treated as evidence that
.B refs/sg/chunks
has been lost, and every chunk pointer in the repository is then treated as
broken rather than silently re-interpreted as ordinary content. It should
not be hand-edited.
.RE
.TP
.B refs/sg/chunks
An
.B sg
specific keep-alive ref (a commit, not a branch or tag) that references
every chunk blob any file in this repository has ever been split into.
Its sole purpose is to keep chunk blobs reachable from git's object graph
so that
.B git gc
(run by real
.B git
or by any other tool) does not collect them as garbage \(em without it, a
chunk pointer blob's referenced chunks could be silently deleted out from
under it.
.B Must not be deleted once chunking has been used
in a repository: if
.B refs/sg/chunks
is deleted or otherwise lost while
.B everchunked
is still recorded as true, every chunked file becomes unrecoverable and
.B sg
fails hard (rather than silently returning pointer text as file content)
for each one. It is merged, not overwritten, by
.BR clone / fetch ,
and its update is bundled together with the branch ref update by
.B push
when the server supports atomic ref updates.
.TP
.B .git/sg-chunks.lock
A lock file (created with
.B O_CREAT|O_EXCL
and never read) used to serialize concurrent updates to
.B refs/sg/chunks
across concurrent
.B sg
invocations in the same repository (e.g. two
.B "sg add"
calls on different large files, or an
.B add
racing a
.BR fetch ).
Only its existence is meaningful, not its content. A lock held
by a process that was killed or crashed can be left behind; if
.B sg
repeatedly fails to acquire it (after roughly 4 seconds of retries), remove
it by hand once you have confirmed no other
.B sg
process is actually running against the repository.
Concurrency protection is local-machine only \(em it is not safe against
concurrent
.B sg
processes on different machines sharing the repository over a network
filesystem.
.TP
.B refs/small-git/undo/*
Where
.B sg
stores its automatic safety snapshots (see the
.B undo
command above), one ref per snapshot. Not intended to be read or edited
directly; use
.B "sg undo"
to list and restore them.
.TP
.B .git/sg-rebase/
Holds the on-disk state of an in-progress
.B sg rebase
(the original branch/commit, the upstream target, and the remaining
todo list). Present only while a rebase is paused or running; removed
automatically when the rebase finishes, is skipped to completion, or is
aborted. Not intended to be edited by hand \(em use
.BR "sg rebase --continue|--skip|--abort" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
.TQ
.B GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
Identity recorded as both the author and (unless overridden below) the
committer of commits created by
.BR commit ,
.BR merge ,
and
.BR rebase ,
and of the internal keep-alive commits chunked storage writes to
.BR refs/sg/chunks .
Default when unset or empty:
.B small_git
and
.BR sg@localhost ,
respectively. There is no config-file equivalent (no
.BR user.name / user.email
support) \(em these environment variables are the only way to set commit
identity.
.TP
.B GIT_COMMITTER_NAME
.TQ
.B GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL
Committer identity used specifically for the internal keep-alive commits
that chunked storage writes to
.BR refs/sg/chunks ;
defaults to the same value as
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
/
.B GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
if unset. Ordinary commits made by
.BR commit / merge / rebase
always use
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
/
.B GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
for the committer field too (there is no separate committer override for
those).
.TP
.B SG_USERNAME
.TQ
.B SG_PASSWORD
HTTP basic-auth credentials used by
.BR clone ,
.BR fetch ,
and
.B push
when talking to a remote over
.BR http(s) .
If unset, credentials are still looked up from
.B ~/.netrc
(via libcurl's optional netrc support) for a matching host; a 401/403
response suggests both of these. There is no
.B sg
specific mechanism for token/OAuth authentication beyond what a URL's own
userinfo or these two variables provide.
.PP
.B sg
does not read or honor any proxy- or TLS-verification-specific environment
variable itself (no
.B SG_PROXY,
no
.B SG_NO_VERIFY_SSL,
etc.); TLS certificate verification is always left at libcurl's secure
defaults and is not configurable via
.B sg .
The underlying HTTP transport (libcurl) does, independently of
.BR sg 's
own code, honor the standard
.B http_proxy
/
.B https_proxy
/
.B no_proxy
environment variables if libcurl was built with that support, exactly as
.B curl(1)
itself would.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: an invalid invocation (bad flags/argument count), a repository
state that prevents the operation (dirty working directory, unresolved
conflicts, not a repository, etc.), an aborted confirmation prompt, an I/O
error, or a merge/rebase left paused on a conflict.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1)
.PP
.BR docs/DESIGN.md
in the small_git source tree documents the project's design goals,
phased roadmap, and per-phase known limitations in more detail than this
manual page.
.SH BUGS
.B sg
implements a deliberately narrow subset of git's functionality (see
.B DESCRIPTION
above and small_git's
.B README.md
\(dq已知限制\(dq / Known Limitations section); missing flags and
subcommands found in real git are not oversights to be reported unless
they contradict what is documented here.
